The observable effect depends on configured provider base URLs and model IDs, none of whose deployed values are supplied. The diff establishes the conditional mechanisms but not whether any real installation exercises them.

DeepSeek compatibility is broadened in two ways: OpenAI-compatible endpoints whose configured base URL contains "deepseek" now use the standard OpenAI SDK client even when the URL also looks Azure-hosted, and model IDs containing "deepseek-r1" are now classified like existing DeepSeek reasoner models in both provider handlers.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When OpenAiHandler is configured with a base URL containing "deepseek" case-insensitively, it now constructs the standard OpenAI client. This check precedes the Azure-domain check, so a URL containing both "deepseek" and "azure.com" also takes the standard OpenAI client path rather than the AzureOpenAI path.
  • Models whose IDs contain "deepseek-r1" are now classified as DeepSeek reasoner models by both DeepSeekHandler and OpenAiHandler, causing them to enter the same subsequent reasoner-specific handling as IDs containing "deepseek-reasoner".

